<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the best ski boot is the one that fits your foot. Get your foot measured in mondopoint (centimeters), choose a last width that matches your foot, pick a flex for your ability and weight, and have a boot fitter check the shell fit. A snug, painless fit matters more than any feature.</p></div>

<h2>Why boots matter most</h2>
<p>Boots connect you to your skis. Every movement you make goes through them, so a boot that's too big makes the ski feel vague and hard to control, and one that's too tight makes for a miserable day. If you buy only one piece of gear, buy boots. See <%= link_to "rent or buy ski gear", guide_path("rent-or-buy-ski-gear") %> for why boots come first.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Measure your foot (mondopoint)</h2>
<p>Ski boots are sized in mondopoint, which is your foot length in centimeters. A foot that measures 27 cm long is a size 27 or 27.5. Measure both feet standing up, heel against a wall, and use the longer foot. A shop can measure length and width for you in a couple of minutes.</p>
<p>Don't assume your street shoe size converts directly. Ski boots should fit much closer than shoes.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Choose the last width</h2>
<p>The last is the width of the boot at the widest part of your forefoot, in millimeters. Boots are usually grouped like this:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Narrow:</strong> about 97–98 mm, for low-volume feet. Precise, but tight for many people.</li>
  <li><strong>Medium:</strong> about 100 mm, fits the widest range of feet.</li>
  <li><strong>Wide:</strong> about 102 mm and up, for wider feet and comfort-focused skiers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pick the narrowest last you can wear comfortably. Shells can be stretched in specific spots, but a boot that's too wide everywhere can't be made smaller.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Pick a flex</h2>
<p>Flex ratings describe how stiff the boot is when you lean forward. Ratings vary between brands, so use them as a guide rather than an exact number.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Softer flex:</strong> forgiving and comfortable. Best for beginners and lighter skiers.</li>
  <li><strong>Medium flex:</strong> for intermediates who ski most of the mountain.</li>
  <li><strong>Stiffer flex:</strong> for advanced and expert skiers, heavier skiers, and aggressive skiing. Too stiff and you can't flex the boot, which makes skiing harder.</li>
</ul>
<p>Women's boots are typically designed with a different cuff shape and flex range. Choose whichever fits your foot and calf best.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Check the fit</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Shell fit:</strong> take the liner out, put your foot in the shell, and slide your toes to the front. The gap behind your heel should be small, roughly one to two fingers. A fitter will do this for you.</li>
  <li><strong>In the boot:</strong> with the liner in and thin ski socks on, buckle up. Standing straight, your toes should lightly touch the front.</li>
  <li><strong>Flex forward:</strong> bend your knees into a skiing stance. Your heel should stay down and your toes should pull back slightly from the front.</li>
  <li><strong>Wear them:</strong> keep them on for 10–15 minutes in the shop. Numbness or sharp pressure points are warning signs.</li>
</ol>
<p>Liners pack out with use, so a boot that's slightly snug in the shop usually feels right after a few days on snow. A boot that feels roomy in the shop will feel big later.</p>

<h2>Features worth knowing</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Heat-moldable liners and shells:</strong> a fitter can shape them to your foot for a more custom fit.</li>
  <li><strong>Walk mode:</strong> a lever that frees the cuff for easier walking. Useful for long walks from the parking lot or for touring.</li>
  <li><strong>Sole type:</strong> boot soles come in different standards (alpine, GripWalk, touring). Make sure your boot sole is compatible with your bindings.</li>
  <li><strong>Custom footbeds:</strong> supportive insoles can improve comfort and control, especially if you have high arches or flat feet.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where to buy</h2>
<p>A boot fitter at a ski shop is worth the visit, especially for your first pair. If you shop online, measure carefully and look for retailers with easy returns. Browse <%= gear_link "ski boots", "ski boots" %>, and pair them with proper <%= gear_link "ski socks", "ski socks" %>: one thin pair, never cotton. Our guide to <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %> covers the rest.</p>
<p>Once you have boots that fit, choosing skis gets easier. Use the <%= link_to "ski size calculator", ski_size_chart_path %> to narrow down length.</p>
